'''''Orobanche hederae''''', the '''ivy broomrape''', is, like other members of the [[genus]] ''[[Orobanche]]'', a parasitic plant without [[chlorophyll]], and thus totally dependent on its host, which is [[Hedera|ivy]]. It grows to {{cvt|60|cm|ft|0}}, with stems in shades of brown and purple, sometimes yellow. The flowers are {{cvt|10|–|22|mm|1}} long, cream in colour with reddish-purple veins.{{r|stac10}}

==Etymology==
''Orobanche'' is derived from Greek, and means 'bitter vetch strangler' or 'legume strangler'. This name originates from the species ''[[Orobanche rapum-genistae]]'', which parasitizes legumes. The name ''hederae'' means 'of ivy', in reference to its host plant, ''Hedera''.{{r|gledhill}}{{r|cretanflora}}

Its common name in English is ''ivy broomrape''.{{r|ecoport}}{{r|mb054}}{{r|plantennamen}}{{r|eppo}} It is also called ''{{lang|ca|frare de l'heura}}'' in Catalan,{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|hr|bršljanov volovod}}'' in Croatian,{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|cs|záraza břečťanová}}'' in Czech,{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|da|vedbend-gyvelkvæler}}'' in Danish,{{r|plantennamen}}{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|nl|klimopbremraap}}'' in Dutch,{{r|mb054}}{{r|plantennamen}}{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|fr|orobanche du lierre}}'' in French,{{r|ecoport}}{{r|mb054}}{{r|plantennamen}}{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|fy|klimmerblêdfretter}}'' in Frysk,{{r|plantennamen}} ''{{lang|de|efeuwürger}}''{{r|eppo}} and ''{{lang|de|efeu-sommerwurz}}'' in German,{{r|mb054}}{{r|plantennamen}}{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|hu|borostyán-vajvirág}}'' in Hungarian,{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|ga|múchóg}}''{{r|eppo}} or ''{{lang|ga|múchóg mhór}}'' in Irish,{{r|wildflowersofireland}} ''{{lang|it|succiamele dell'edera}}'' in Italian,{{r|plantennamen}}{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|no|bergflette-snylterot}}'' in Norwegian,{{r|plantennamen}} ''{{lang|pt|erva-toira da hera}}''{{r|ecoport}} or ''{{lang|pt|erva-toira}}''{{r|eppo}} in Portuguese, ''{{lang|ru|заразиха плющевая}}'' in Russian,{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|sk|záraza brečtanová}}'' in Slovak,{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|sl|pojalnik bršljanov}}'' in Slovene,{{r|mb054}}{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|es|espárrago de lobo}}'', ''{{lang|es|jopo de la hiedra}}'', ''{{lang|es|jopo espárrago de lobo}}'', or ''{{lang|es|matalegumbre}}'' in Spanish,{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|sv|murgrönssnyltrot}}'' in Swedish,{{r|plantennamen}}{{r|eppo}} ''{{lang|tr|tez canavarotu}}'' in Turkish,{{r|eppo}} and ''{{lang|cy|gorfanc eiddew}}'' or ''{{lang|cy|gorfanhadlen eiddew}}'' in Welsh.{{r|mb054}}{{r|eppo}}

==Description==
Its yellowish to purplish stems are usually strongly swollen at the base and {{cvt|15|-|60|cm}} by {{cvt|0.3|-|0.8|cm}}. They are covered in short soft glandular hairs. Leaves are acute and oblong to lance-shaped. Its calyx (sepals) are {{cvt|10|-|15|mm}} with free segments that are entire or unequally bifid. Its dull-cream to reddish purple corolla (petals) is {{cvt|10|-|22|mm}}. They are almost hairless and upright spreading to more or less patent. Filaments (stalks of the stamen) are inserted {{cvt|3|-|4|mm}} above the base of the corolla. They are usually hairless but rarely somewhat hairy below. Fruit are {{cvt|10|-|12|mm}} capsules.{{r|cretanflora}}

Its fruiting spikes contain thousands of minute seeds.{{r|thorogood2021}}

===Identification===
Ivy broomrape closely resembles the more commonly distributed ''[[Orobanche minor|O. minor]]'' and both are able to parasitize members of the ivy family. ''O. hederae'' is primarily differentiated by its characteristically distally pinched corollas, the flowers extending over most of the stem, the long, acuminate floral bracts, and its large terminal 'bud' of unopened flowers that give the spike a pointed top.{{r|bsbi}} The stigma on ''O. hederae'' is usually yellow and the corolla is curved downwards and cream-colored.{{r|botsocscot2020}}

==Ecology==
Ivy broomrape primarily propagates by seeds which are dispersed by wind, but is also able to regenerate from small fragments of roots that remain in the soil.{{r|thorogood2021}}{{r|wildflowerweb}} Many broomrape seeds show considerable abilities to remain dormant but viable for many years.{{r|thorogood2021}}

When germination occurs, the embryo of the seed sends out a thin thread-like filament that spirals away from the embryo and into the surround soils until it makes contacts with ivy roots. The filament then penetrates xylem tissues which allows it to receive water, nutrients, and carbohydrates from its ivy host.{{r|idop}} The connection made with the host can be so complete that its nearly impossible to tell with certainty where the epidermis of the host ends and where that of the parasite begins.{{r|tate1925}} In order for a seed to survive, this connection must occur within a few days of germination.{{r|plants400}} Only once enough energy has been garnered will the plant send up a flower stock and be visible above ground.{{r|idop}}

===Host specificity===
Ivy broomrape is an obligate parasite of [[Hedera|ivy]] (''Hedera'') plants, primarily affecting ''[[Hedera helix|English ivy]]'', but, rarely, other species as well. It contains no chlorophyll of its own{{r|baccarini1967}} and seed germination is dependent on [[strigolactone]] root exudates of the host. Unlike more weedy species of broomrape like [[Orobanche minor|common broomrape]], ''O. hederae'' exhibits very high host specificity based on germination measurements in response to kinds of strigolactone typical of various possible host plants.{{r|fernandezaparicio2010}}

It is one of two parasitic plants associated with common ivy; the other being ''[[Osyris alba]]''.{{r|metcalfe2005}} However, osyris has a very wide host range{{r|metcalfe2005}} while ''O. hederae'''s range is extremely narrow.{{r|fernandezaparicio2010}}

===Seasonality===
Pollen units are medium sized (26-50&nbsp;μm) spheroidal monads.{{r|paldat}} Flowering can take 2 years after infection to occur. However flowering is influenced by or coordinated with their host. Ivy broomrape sown onto an alien host like ''[[Tetrapanax]]'' was shown to flower in a single year.{{r|thorogood2021}} In Mediterranean climates it flowers from late April to mid July.{{r|cretanflora}} In the UK, flowers are generally observed in June and July{{r|wildflowerweb}}, June–August in the Netherlands,{{r|wilde-planten}} and in Ireland flowering generally occurs March–July.{{r|wildflowersofireland}} Flowering can vary widely and some sources even cite its flowering months to range from April to October.{{r|obotanicoaprendiznaterradosespantos}}

==Distribution==
Its native distribution matches that of its host, ivy, so it is mainly found in central and Northern Europe as well as parts of Asia. In the US, it has been observed in a patch of invasive ivy at the University of California, Berkeley, near the life sciences building.{{r|idop}}{{r|wildflowerweb}}

